 "about": "Criteria for what a relevant person is, per query. See grader.py for how they are applied: `strong` subjects in a person's stated topics grade 2; `strong` subjects only in their titles or bio, or `related` subjects in their topics, grade 1. `country` and `org` must hold unless `soft` is set, in which case a person who STATES the subject as a topic grades 1 without them -- two paper titles plus a failed constraint is two levels of weak evidence and grades 0. A soft query nobody meets in full is reported as measuring its subject only, because every relevant person then carries the same gain and the constraint is not being tested. `name` grades by name words. Kinds: topic (a subject the vocabulary holds), concept (needs related subjects understood), agent (a person noun: physicists), constrained, soft (constraints nobody may meet in full), typo, protected, name. Kind holdout: written after the concept work, criteria fixed before running, never tuned against — the check that improvements generalise. Kind holdout2: a second set written after every change listed so far, criteria fixed before its first run, scored once and never tuned against. Kind holdout/holdout2 means criteria fixed before the first run -- EXCEPT where a case carries a `tuned` field, which records that the criterion was edited after results were seen, and why. Such a case is no longer an untouched holdout and its number cannot be read as one.",
